Hospital workers demonstrating in Paris  last week to demand better pay and  conditions the latest in a series of  protests by public sector workers in  France now trade unions want to increase  pressure on the French government  they've called for a nationwide strike  to demand higher salaries and an end to  the government's pension reform plans  this Railway union representative says  people are fed up and inflation's making  things worse  we are in a tense social situation  workers are angry about the costs going  up they see big companies making record  profits but refusing to raise salaries  so going into the streets feels like the  only way to try and force them to  increase our pay the French government  says it's doing all that it can to  Shield people from inflation its capped  energy bills for consumers offered  financial help to poorer households and  is considering a pay rise for teachers  but it is standing firm on its  controversial pension reform plans  to get out of a system in which the same  job doesn't guarantee the same pension I  say yes we will all have to work a bit  longer French president Emmanuel McGraw  has been promising to overhaul France's  complex pension system ever since his  first term in office he says it needs to  be streamlined to be made fairer he also  wants to raise the retirement age from  62 to 64 or 65.  Macross past attempts to change the  retirement age and pension scheme were  met with large protests from people who  said he was eroding their social rights  it's a real worry for him today in this  climate of anxiety inflation and high  energy prices opinion polls show that  French people are against the pension  reform being forced through so quickly  so this could be the start of a protest  movement like the one we saw in macron's  first term with the yellow vests  the pandemic and war in Ukraine knocked  macron's reformist agenda off track he  wants to revive it and fulfill his  campaign promise but forging ahead with  his Ambitions is unlikely to be easy at  a time when people are struggling  financially at the economic Outlook is  so Bleak Natasha Butler Al Jazeera Paris
 

